The DP tracks subarrays ending at the previous index by length parity and deletion usage, plus no-deletion states from two indices back to handle deleting the previous element (which flips position parity for the current element).

```python
class Solution:
    def maxAlternatingSum(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:
        NEG = -10**18
        # States for subarrays ending exactly at the previous index:
        # o0/e0: odd/even length, no deletion used
        # o1/e1: odd/even length, one deletion used
        o0 = e0 = o1 = e1 = NEG
        # No-deletion states ending exactly two indices back (for "delete previous element")
        p_o0 = p_e0 = NEG
        ans = NEG
        for x in nums:
            # No deletion: start fresh at x, or extend an even-length subarray (x at even position)
            no0 = max(x, e0 + x)
            # No deletion: extend an odd-length subarray (x at odd position)
            ne0 = o0 - x
            # One deletion: extend a one-deletion even-length subarray,
            # or delete the previous element so x takes its (even) position
            no1 = max(e1 + x, p_e0 + x)
            # One deletion: extend a one-deletion odd-length subarray,
            # or delete the previous element so x takes its (odd) position
            ne1 = max(o1 - x, p_o0 - x)
            p_o0, p_e0 = o0, e0
            o0, e0, o1, e1 = no0, ne0, no1, ne1
            ans = max(ans, o0, e0, o1, e1)
        return ans
```